WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.235
-Welcome back, Jason.
How are you, buddy?

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:04.603
-Not too bad, you know.

00:00:04.603 --> 00:00:08.707
Healthy, and -- but locked up
and going crazy, like everyone.

00:00:08.707 --> 00:00:12.178
-I think we were going so crazy
and so locked up with our kids

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:14.247
that we took the step of

00:00:14.247 --> 00:00:15.715
actually using them
in a sketch.

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:16.983
We did
a "Second Chance Theater,"

00:00:16.983 --> 00:00:18.751
which now seems like
a long time ago.

00:00:18.751 --> 00:00:21.821
-I know.
-And I could only use,

00:00:21.821 --> 00:00:22.989
in the end, one of my kids.

00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:24.791
Axel, it turned out,
was too young to act.

00:00:24.791 --> 00:00:26.025
So Ashe was in it,

00:00:26.025 --> 00:00:28.828
but both Daisy and Otis
came through big time.

00:00:28.828 --> 00:00:30.763
Are they different actors?

00:00:30.763 --> 00:00:33.400
Are they different
kinds of performers?

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.668
-Very different.
Very different process.

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:38.772
Yeah, Otis is, like,
a premise guy.

00:00:38.772 --> 00:00:40.372
He's like one of those kids

00:00:40.372 --> 00:00:42.408
that I had friends
growing up like that.

00:00:42.408 --> 00:00:45.078
They're like, "Hey, what
if Mr. Doyle did this?"

00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:46.579
You know, and you just sort of
sit in the back of

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:48.515
the classroom, and you're kind
of pretending,

00:00:48.515 --> 00:00:50.983
"What if we threw something
into the fan?"

00:00:50.983 --> 00:00:52.752
And Otis is that guy.

00:00:52.752 --> 00:00:55.421
And then Daisy is just full-on
Daniel Day-Lewis.

00:00:55.421 --> 00:00:57.056
She's just in it the whole time.

00:00:57.056 --> 00:00:59.892
She wakes up Olivia
almost every morning,

00:00:59.892 --> 00:01:03.829
coming into our room going,
"Mama, will you pretend

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:08.334
I'm a baby horse named,"
you know, "Screwdriver?"

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:11.737
And then, Liv is, "Okay, yeah.
Come here, sweetie."

00:01:11.737 --> 00:01:13.173
And then she crawls into bed.

00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:15.708
And then, she just, "Mm, mm"
and then, does the same thing.

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:19.512
But she will stay in it forever
until you yell "cut,"

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:21.447
and then sometimes beyond.

00:01:21.447 --> 00:01:24.517
-She definitely -- She was
the standout of the three.

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:27.620
I will willingly admit that.
-Yeah.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:31.290
-Your wife Olivia also had
the idea -- this was --

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.760
Ted Lasso was a character you
did in a series of commercials

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:36.663
for NBC Sports
a few years back now.

00:01:36.663 --> 00:01:39.966
And Olivia was the one who
suggested that maybe this could

00:01:39.966 --> 00:01:42.268
expand into, you know,
the kind of character

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:44.203
that could carry a show.
-Yeah.

00:01:44.203 --> 00:01:47.640
And I totally forgot about that
until Brendan mentioned it,

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.275
our buddy, Brendan Hunt,
who is on the show

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:50.543
and was one of
the producers/writers.

00:01:50.543 --> 00:01:51.845
He mentioned it in the press,

00:01:51.845 --> 00:01:53.378
and then Liv mentioned it to me
at home.

00:01:53.378 --> 00:01:55.248
I was like, "Really?"

00:01:55.248 --> 00:01:56.749
She was like, "Yeah."
We were out to dinner.

00:01:56.749 --> 00:01:58.050
And she explained
the whole thing to me,

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:00.053
and she just liked the fact
that we enjoyed doing it,

00:02:00.053 --> 00:02:02.489
loved working with, you know,
our buddies Brendan and Joe.

00:02:02.489 --> 00:02:06.292
And similar to the reason I
enjoy doing it because it's --

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:07.960
he's just in a good mood
a lot of times.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:09.528
Especially by the time we did
the second commercial,

00:02:09.528 --> 00:02:11.430
and she was like,
"You should look at doing this

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:12.564
as a TV show or a movie."

00:02:12.564 --> 00:02:13.966
And then she reminded me
of that.

00:02:13.966 --> 00:02:15.835
I just sort of sat there at
dinner and just started riffing.

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:17.469
I was like, "Why is he so happy?

00:02:17.469 --> 00:02:19.171
Oh, maybe he's going
through something."

00:02:19.171 --> 00:02:21.974
And then I kind of just went in
my head and ruminated over,

00:02:21.974 --> 00:02:24.010
you know, deviled eggs
or who knows what, some --

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:25.444
back when you could go
to restaurants.

00:02:25.444 --> 00:02:27.313
Anything's possible.

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:29.749
-And this was --
you know, I really --

00:02:29.749 --> 00:02:32.752
I love the commercials, and they
definitely played off the idea

00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:35.121
of a fish out of water,
an American football coach

00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:39.358
who is in charge of
a British soccer team.

00:02:39.358 --> 00:02:44.931
I will say I was surprised
and delighted by the change

00:02:44.931 --> 00:02:46.465
in sort of tone of this show.

00:02:46.465 --> 00:02:48.334
I mean, there's still
the fish-out-of-water quality,

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:51.303
but you have
a very upbeat, optimistic,

00:02:51.303 --> 00:02:53.405
sort of Jimmy Stewart-type
attitude about

00:02:53.405 --> 00:02:55.307
what you're embarking on
as this coach.

00:02:55.307 --> 00:02:56.876
-High praise, yeah.

00:02:56.876 --> 00:02:59.578
I really do believe that that
happened from getting to do

00:02:59.578 --> 00:03:01.414
with the second commercial
because the first commercial,

00:03:01.414 --> 00:03:03.482
the whole premise was
that he gets hired by

00:03:03.482 --> 00:03:05.851
the Tottenham Hotspurs and then
gets fired three days later.

00:03:05.851 --> 00:03:07.920
And so when those commercials
did well, they asked us to do

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.888
a second one, but they're like,
"But you can't go to the U.K.

00:03:09.888 --> 00:03:10.923
We don't have the budget
for that."

00:03:10.923 --> 00:03:12.258
It was like, "Okay. Alright."

00:03:12.258 --> 00:03:14.260
Well, then, maybe he just
loved his brief time there

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:15.528
and fell in love with the sport.

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:17.463
And it was that, like,
childlike enthusiasm

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:20.633
that really unlocked it for me
and made it really fun to play.

00:03:20.633 --> 00:03:22.167
And I just think him being

00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:24.303
relentlessly stupid
would be impossible.

00:03:24.303 --> 00:03:25.905
Our big rule in
the writers' room was like,

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:28.374
"He's seen 'SportsCenter.'

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:31.010
You know, like, "He knows
the ball has to go in the net."

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:34.380
-I do like that he is a --
It shows that you can be cheery

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.248
and optimistic and also
not stupid

00:03:36.248 --> 00:03:41.553
because even when people make
fun of him, he is both cheery,

00:03:41.553 --> 00:03:44.457
and you can tell on your face
as an actor, is aware

00:03:44.457 --> 00:03:46.092
that he has just been burned.

00:03:46.092 --> 00:03:47.160
-Yes.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.729
-I like that depth of character.
-Thanks.

00:03:49.729 --> 00:03:52.065
-It really, like, steers away
from being cartoonish at all,

00:03:52.065 --> 00:03:54.800
which I think is just fantastic.

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:58.804
-Yeah, the mustache is
about as cartoony as it gets.

00:03:58.804 --> 00:04:02.808
We really -- But, yeah, that was
a big thing for us, was --

00:04:02.808 --> 00:04:05.844
I also love that -- do you
remember that Del Close quote,

00:04:05.844 --> 00:04:07.680
you know, our improv guru?

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.615
Did you have him
when you were in Chicago? Yeah.

00:04:09.615 --> 00:04:11.217
-I missed him. I missed him.
-Yeah.

00:04:11.217 --> 00:04:12.484
Yeah, you were in --
you were at Boom.

00:04:12.484 --> 00:04:13.519
You were in Amsterdam
at that time

00:04:13.519 --> 00:04:15.121
when he was doing his thing.
-Yeah.

00:04:15.121 --> 00:04:17.790
-But he would say, "Treat your
audience like poets and geniuses

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:19.258
and they'll rise to
the occasion."

00:04:19.258 --> 00:04:23.896
And I feel like, you know,
Ted -- it's nice to know that

00:04:23.896 --> 00:04:25.031
it is coming across that,

00:04:25.031 --> 00:04:26.532
yeah, Ted knows
he's being insulted.

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:28.701
He just -- He just knows that
it's -- Well, they don't know me

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:31.603
well enough to really be
talking about me.

00:04:31.603 --> 00:04:33.039
You know, the other thing
we talk about is, like,

00:04:33.039 --> 00:04:34.273
Ted is kind of like mushrooms.

00:04:34.273 --> 00:04:35.374
He's sort of egoless,

00:04:35.374 --> 00:04:37.176
and people put
their own thing on him,

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:40.546
and he's just kind of like,
"Yeah, alright. Well, okay."

00:04:40.546 --> 00:04:43.482
It just bounces off him
because, again,

00:04:43.482 --> 00:04:45.918
if you don't know someone,
how can you hate them already?

00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:47.953
It's not for the right reasons.
It's their own stuff,

00:04:47.953 --> 00:04:50.323
and it doesn't rattle him in
the least because, you know,

00:04:50.323 --> 00:04:52.258
he's kind of like, "Oh, you
know, I'll find my way here."

